From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomtomnet <tompere8000@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 17:58:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ui/sdl2: frame and input latency stats
 (x-query-display-stats)

The SDL display now measures the guest frames it shows, and the new QMP
command x-query-display-stats (unstable) returns, for each console, over
the last second:

- guest flushes, frames presented, dropped, scanned out directly and
  redrawn, per second;
- frame latency: from QEMU receiving the guest's flush (dpy_gl_update or
  a 2D update) to the compositor showing that frame, and QEMU's share of
  it, from the flush to the end of the buffer swap;
- the interval between frames on screen;
- input latency: from a key press, click or wheel turn to the guest's
  first frame on screen after the event reached it, over the last 10 s.
  It counts only when the guest had flushed nothing for the 50 ms before,
  so that the frame is likely its answer. Also how long input waited
  before the display read it;
- the guest size, the monitor's refresh rate, and how the end times are
  known: "presentation", "swap" or "none".

Each duration comes as samples, median, 99th percentile and maximum, in
microseconds.

On Wayland a frame ends at the compositor's own presentation time: QEMU
asks wp_presentation for feedback on SDL's wl_surface right before each
swap, so that the swap's commit carries the request, and dispatches the
replies on an event queue of its own after SDL has read the compositor's
events. Elsewhere, X11 and XWayland included, the latency ends when the
swap returns. Every flush is numbered, so the flushes between two frames
on screen that the second does not show count as dropped, whether QEMU
merged them into one redraw or the compositor replaced them.

Meson looks for wayland-client and wayland-scanner, with no option, and
generates the client code from a bundled presentation-time.xml; the
summary says "SDL Wayland presentation feedback". Without them QEMU
builds as before and falls back to swap times. Other displays return an
empty list of consoles. -trace 'sdl2_stats*' logs every flush, swap,
presentation and input event with CLOCK_MONOTONIC times. The README says
what the numbers mean; qemu-gui-manager shows them in its status bar.

Checked against KWin's own frame log (KWIN_LOG_PERFORMANCE_DATA): every
presentation time QEMU received is a KWin page flip, and predicting each
frame from KWin's log alone gives the same flip for 3016 of 3020 frames
at 240 Hz, the other 4 one refresh apart. The main thread's CPU time per
frame is unchanged within noise (955 us, against 952 us without). A 2D
virtio-vga guest flipping at 240 Hz shows 239 of its 240 frames per
second on KWin, with a frame latency of 10.4 ms, of which QEMU 0.2 ms.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8iTzFPtyk2Gzbt6CLnRYK
